Subject: [r-inla] Modelling quadratic relationships using a squared copy
of random effects
Date: Thu, 28 Apr 2022 01:33:31 -0700 (PDT)

Hi,
I am wondering if it is possible to squared a copy of a random effect.

I am working on a joint spatial model to deal with spatial misalignment.
I want to model a quadratic relationship between a Poisson response
variable Y (species abundance) and a Normal covariate X (like
temperature) that are spatially misaligned, Y and X have been sampled at
different locations.
Thus, I use a spatial joint model that first predict covariate values at
response locations, and then use those predicted values (pred.X) to
estimate coefficient parameters describing the relationship between X
and Y.
For a linear relationship between predicted X and Y (Y ~ b0 + b1 *
pred.X) the code for the formula used in inla() looks like this:

    formula <- y ~ -1 +
      f(X.field, model = spde) +
      intercept +
      f(predX.field,
        copy = "X.field",
        fixed = FALSE,
        hyper = list(prec = list(param = prior.b)))

Is it possible to extend this for a quadratic relationship between
predicted X and observed Y (Y ~ b0 + b1 * pred.X + b2 * (pred.X^2))?
Something that could look like:

    formula <- y ~ -1 +
      f(X.field, model = spde) +
      intercept +
      f(predX.field,
        copy = "X.field",
        fixed = FALSE,
        hyper = list(prec = list(param = prior.b))) +
      f(I(predX.field ^ 2),
        copy = "X.field",
        fixed = FALSE,
        hyper = list(prec = list(param = prior.b)))
Any ideas will be appreciated.

Thank you for your time.
Bastien

