I think I've got closer.
What I think is happening, and please forgive me if I've misunderstood
something, is that when eigenvalues of the Hessian are negative, you get
marginal.hyperpars of Inf 0.000000e+00. When we're then applying
inla.tmarginal() and taking the square of those values root, all breaks
down.

> phylo_effect_iid_model = inla.tmarginal(function(x) 1/sqrt(x),
+
output$marginals.hyperpar$`Precision for phy_id_iid_model`,
+                                         method = "linear") %>%
+     inla.qmarginal(c(0.025, 0.5, 0.975), .)
Error in seq.default(xmin, xmax, len = n) :
  'from' must be a finite number

Does that make sense?

What I've now done is check for Inf values and if they occur,  not apply
this inla.tmarginal() and render a df from it but just make a df with
the same cols but with NA where the margins would have been.

https://github.com/HedvigS/INLA_problem_binary_variables/blob/main/INLA_featurewise.R#L255

Not the prettiest solution, I agree and perhaps I'll think of something
more smart tmrw. Of course, I need to get to the bottom of why we're
getting negative eigenvalues in the Hessian at all

/Hedvig

