> > > Based on your prediction table, a mean of 25000 corresponds to a
> > > median of 1000, so assuming a normal posterior on the log-scale
> > > (so that the log-scale mean and median are the same), we get
> > > exp(log(1000) + s^2/2) = 25000, where s is the posterior std.dev <http://std.dev>
> > > on the log-scale.
> > > That gives
> > > s = sqrt(2*log(25000/1000)) = 2.54 and log(1000) = 6.9 as the the
> > > log-scale median (and mean)
> > >
> > > These look like quite reasonable values on their own, but it shows
> > > that 2.54 is quite a large standard deviation on the log-scale.
> > > So one place to look for problems is in how you choose your prior
> > > distributions for standard deviations and precisions!
